The Rule of Zero is the modern C++ guideline that says:

If your class does not directly own a resource, don't write any of the special member functions. Let the compiler generate them.

This is the preferred approach in modern C++ because resource management is delegated to RAII types such as std::unique_ptr, 
std::shared_ptr, std::vector, and std::string.

=========================================================
Rule of Zero (C++11) – Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is Rule of Zero?
------------------------
If a class does not directly manage a resource,
do NOT write:

• Destructor
• Copy Constructor
• Copy Assignment Operator
• Move Constructor
• Move Assignment Operator

Let the compiler generate them automatically.

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why Rule of Zero?
--------------------
Use RAII classes provided by the STL.

Examples:
• std::vector
• std::string
• std::unique_ptr
• std::shared_ptr

These classes already manage resources correctly.

---------------------------------------------------------

3. Example
----------

class Buffer
{
private:
    std::vector<int> data;

public:
    Buffer(int size)
        : data(size)
    {
    }
};

No destructor.
No copy constructor.
No assignment operators.
No move constructor.

Compiler-generated versions are sufficient.

---------------------------------------------------------

4. Why?
-------

std::vector already performs:
• Deep copy
• Move semantics
• Automatic cleanup
• Exception safety

Therefore your class doesn't need to.

---------------------------------------------------------

5. Rule of Three vs Five vs Zero

Rule of Three
--------------
If managing a resource manually,
implement:
• Destructor
• Copy Constructor
• Copy Assignment

Rule of Five
-------------
In C++11+, also implement:
• Move Constructor
• Move Assignment

Rule of Zero
-------------
Don't manage resources manually.
Use RAII classes and let the compiler
generate everything.

---------------------------------------------------------

6. Example using unique_ptr

class Employee
{
private:
    std::unique_ptr<int> salary;

public:
    Employee()
        : salary(std::make_unique<int>(100))
    {
    }
};

No destructor needed.

---------------------------------------------------------

7. Interview Questions
----------------------

Q. Which rule should be preferred?

A.
Rule of Zero.

Modern C++ encourages using standard RAII classes
instead of managing resources manually.

Q. When should Rule of Five be used?

A.
Only when your class directly owns resources
using APIs like new/delete, malloc/free,
or other non-RAII resources.

---------------------------------------------------------

8. One-Line Interview Answer
----------------------------

"Rule of Zero means classes should not directly manage resources.
Instead, use RAII types like std::vector, std::string, and
std::unique_ptr, allowing the compiler-generated special member
functions to handle copying, moving, and destruction."

=========================================================
Interview Summary
=========================================================

Rule of Three  → Manual resource management (C++03)

Rule of Five   → Manual resource management + Move semantics (C++11)

Rule of Zero   → No manual resource management; rely on RAII types
                 and compiler-generated special member functions.
